---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Trace1 = `let a = 3;
let b = 4;
let c = a + b * 2;

console.log(c);`;
const Trace2 = `let total = 0;

for (let i = 1; i <= 4; i++)
{
  if (i % 2 === 0)
  {
    total = total + i;
  }
}

console.log(total);`;
const Trace3 = `let items = ["a", "b", "c"];
let out = "";

for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++)
{
  out = items[i] + out;
}

console.log(out);`;
const Trace4 = `nums ← [5, 10, 15]
total ← 0

FOR EACH n IN nums
{
  total ← total + n
}

DISPLAY(total / LENGTH(nums))`;
const Trace5 = `let a = [1, 2, 3];
let b = a;
b[0] = 99;

console.log(a[0]);`;
const Trace6 = `list ← [1, 2, 3]
copy ← list
copy[1] ← 99

DISPLAY(list[1])`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.27: Quarter 1 Review</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.27</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Quarter 1 Review</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What You Can Now Do</h2>
              <p>Store and change data</p>
              <p>Make decisions</p>
              <p>Repeat work</p>
              <p>Hold many values at once</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Three Building Blocks</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Sequencing</dt>
                  <dd>One statement after another, in order</dd>
                  <dt>Selection</dt>
                  <dd>Choosing a path with if, else if, else</dd>
                  <dt>Iteration</dt>
                  <dd>Repeating with while and for</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Three Building Blocks</h2>
              <p>
                <b class="text-sn-theme">
                  Every algorithm on the AP exam is built from those three.
                </b>{" "}
                There is no fourth, and you have all of them.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Next quarter adds ways to <i>organize</i> them, not new ones.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Bugs You Have Met</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <ul>
                  <li class="fragment">Uncast <CodeSnippet code="+" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> joining instead of adding</li>
                  <li class="fragment"><CodeSnippet code="NaN" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> spreading from a failed conversion</li>
                  <li class="fragment">Unreachable branches from a badly ordered chain</li>
                  <li class="fragment">Infinite loops with no change inside</li>
                  <li class="fragment">A maximum started at 0, on negative data</li>
                  <li class="fragment">Off-by-one, walking one step past the end</li>
                  <li class="fragment">Two names, one array</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Not one of those seven produces an error
                message.</b> That is the real lesson of the quarter.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Exam Notation, Assembled</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <ul>
                  <li class="fragment"><CodeSnippet code="←" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> assigns, <CodeSnippet code="=" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> compares</li>
                  <li class="fragment"><CodeSnippet code="MOD" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, <CodeSnippet code="AND" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, <CodeSnippet code="OR" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, <CodeSnippet code="NOT" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /></li>
                  <li class="fragment">No <CodeSnippet code="ELSE IF" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> &mdash; chains nest</li>
                  <li class="fragment"><CodeSnippet code="REPEAT UNTIL" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> inverts the condition</li>
                  <li class="fragment"><CodeSnippet code="REPEAT n TIMES" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> has no counter</li>
                  <li class="fragment">Lists start at <b>1</b>, and <CodeSnippet code="←" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> copies them</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today</h2>
              <p>
                Six traces, then the checkpoint quiz.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                The traces are not graded. Get them wrong now rather than in the
                quiz, and definitely rather than in May.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Warm-up traces -->
        <section id="traces">
          <h2>Warm-Up: Six Traces</h2>

          <p>
            Work each one out <b>on paper</b>. Do not run them. Write down your
            answer before scrolling to the next.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three are JavaScript and three are exam notation. Notice which ones
            make you slow down.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 1 &mdash; JavaScript</h3>
          <Code
            code={Trace1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Trace 1"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 2 &mdash; JavaScript</h3>
          <Code
            code={Trace2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Trace 2"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 3 &mdash; JavaScript</h3>
          <p>Read the accumulator line carefully. The order is not what you expect.</p>
          <Code
            code={Trace3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Trace 3"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 4 &mdash; Exam Notation</h3>
          <Code
            code={Trace4}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Trace 4"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 5 &mdash; JavaScript</h3>
          <Code
            code={Trace5}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Trace 5"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 6 &mdash; Exam Notation</h3>
          <p>
            This is Trace 5 written in exam notation. <b>The answer is different.</b>{" "}
            If you get the same value for both, re-read Activity 1.23.
          </p>
          <Code
            code={Trace6}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Trace 6"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checklist -->
        <section id="checklist">
          <h2>Before the Quiz</h2>

          <p>
            You should be able to do every one of these without looking anything
            up. If one is shaky, that is where to spend your revision.
          </p>

          <h4>Data</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Declare a variable and change it later</li>
            <li>Tell a string, a number, and a boolean apart</li>
            <li>Say why input arrives as text, and cast it</li>
            <li>Explain what <code>NaN</code> means and where it comes from</li>
            <li>Format a number for display without breaking later arithmetic</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Decisions</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Write <code>if</code>, <code>else if</code>, and <code>else</code></li>
            <li>Order a chain so no branch is unreachable</li>
            <li>Combine conditions with <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code>, and <code>!</code></li>
            <li>Choose between nesting and AND, and say why</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Repetition</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Write a <code>while</code> loop and a <code>for</code> loop</li>
            <li>Name the setup, test, and change in either one</li>
            <li>Explain what makes a loop infinite</li>
            <li>Use an accumulator, a counter, and a running maximum</li>
            <li>Predict how many times a nested loop's body runs</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Arrays</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Create an array and read or change an element</li>
            <li>Reach the last element using <code>length</code></li>
            <li>Traverse with an index and with <code>for...of</code>, and pick the right one</li>
            <li>Check an index is valid before using it</li>
            <li>Explain why two names can share one array</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Exam Notation</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Read <code>←</code> and know <code>=</code> means equality</li>
            <li>Rewrite an <code>else if</code> chain as nested <code>IF</code>/<code>ELSE</code></li>
            <li>Flip a <code>while</code> condition into a <code>REPEAT UNTIL</code></li>
            <li>Hand-roll a counter inside <code>REPEAT n TIMES</code></li>
            <li>Index a list from 1, and know that <code>←</code> copies it</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint Quiz</h2>

          <p>
            The Quarter 1 checkpoint is written in <b>exam format</b> &mdash;
            multiple choice, some questions in reference-sheet notation, timed.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is worth considerably more than a homework question set, and it is
            the closest thing to a real AP question you will have seen. That is
            deliberate: a checkpoint score is meant to tell you something honest
            about where you stand.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Everything from Activities 1.1 through 1.26 is fair game</li>
            <li>Some questions show code in exam notation rather than JavaScript</li>
            <li>At least one asks you to select all that apply</li>
            <li>No computer. Work on paper, as you did with the traces</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Pace yourself.</b> If a question is taking
            too long, mark it, move on, and come back. Leaving one blank to answer
            three others is the right trade, and it is a skill worth practicing
            now.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these after the quiz, honestly. They are not graded on being flattering.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Which of the seven bugs from the slides have you personally
              written? Pick the one that took you longest to find and describe how
              you found it.
            </li>
            <li>
              Not one of those seven produces an error message. What habit have
              you picked up this quarter that helps you catch that kind of bug?
            </li>
            <li>
              Which part of the exam notation still feels least natural, and what
              would help?
            </li>
            <li>
              Quarter 2 starts with procedures &mdash; packaging code so it can be
              reused with different values. Looking back at your Quarter 1
              programs, where would that have saved you the most repetition?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your reflection to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Quarter 1 Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
